{"challenges":[{"attack":"Could cancellation be invalid because x-2 may be zero?","claim_id":"simplification","outcome":"survives","result":"domain-check establishes x != 2 before factor-cancel cancels x-2, so the cancelled factor is nonzero.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Could x=2 belong to the original domain through a removable discontinuity?","claim_id":"domain","outcome":"survives","result":"The displayed original expression is a quotient with denominator x-2, which is zero at x=2; a later simplification does not change that original domain.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Could agreement for every x != 2 establish equivalence at every real number?","claim_id":"not-global-equivalence","outcome":"survives","result":"No. At x=2 the original expression is undefined, while x+2 is defined and equals 4, so their domains differ.","tool_ids":[]}],"checks":[{"basis_reasoning":"The question explicitly gives the expression and restricts attention to real x where it is defined.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":[],"claim_id":"original-expression","reasoning":"The claim accurately restates the question premise.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"factor-cancel factors x^2-4 and invokes domain-check before cancelling the nonzero factor x-2.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["domain-check","factor-cancel"],"claim_id":"simplification","reasoning":"For each real x != 2, ((x-2)(x+2))/(x-2)=x+2. The quantifier and cancellation restriction are explicit.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"domain-check gives the denominator condition x-2 != 0 exactly when x != 2.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["domain-check"],"claim_id":"domain","reasoning":"For real inputs, the quotient is defined exactly outside 2, hence its domain is R \\ {2}.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"comparison-at-two depends on both the domain restriction and the simplification, then evaluates x+2 at 2.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["domain-check","factor-cancel","comparison-at-two"],"claim_id":"not-global-equivalence","reasoning":"The expressions agree on the original domain but not as expressions defined at every real number, since only x+2 has a value at 2.","verdict":"supported"}],"missing_evidence":[],"recommended_action":"finish","tool_requests":[]}
